    dl_detail: "Detailed check", dl_detail_sub: "result of every pixel: raster, polygons, Google Earth", dd_t_flood: "Flood detailed check", dd_t_fire: "Wildfire detailed check", dd_t_dust: "Dust storm detailed check", dd_p_flood: "Radar images: before the flood {b}, after the flood {a} (UTC)", dd_p_fire: "Pre-fire images {b0} – {b1}, post-fire images {a0} – {a1}", dd_p_dust: "Day checked {d}; Sentinel-2 image dates: {i}", dd_nd_flood: "In the class raster 0 = no flood found. It is marked NoData so it shows transparent in GIS; it does not mean missing data.", dd_nd_fire: "In the class raster 0–6 are the severity classes (table above), 255 = cloud or no image (NoData).", dd_nd_dust: "In the class raster 0 = no image (NoData), 6 = cloud.", dd_c_none: "No flood found", dd_c_nodata: "No image", dd_b_perm: "permanent water (JRC, more than 10 months a year): 1 = yes, 0 = no", dd_b_s2: "new water on Sentinel-2: 1 = water, 0 = not water, −9999 = cloud", dd_b_dnbr: "dNBR = pre-fire NBR − post-fire NBR; burned from 0.10, −9999 = cloud", dd_b_modis: "MODIS MCD64A1 burned area (500 m): 1 = burned, 0 = not", dd_b_fire: "MODIS MOD14A1/MYD14A1 active fire (1 km): 1 = fire found, 0 = not", dd_e_flood_app: "Against the Google Earth Engine App on three floods, the radar flood area of this page differed by 2–24% (Selenge 26.2 and 24.5, Kherlen 1.37 and 1.4, Orkhon 1.98 and 2.6 km²).", dd_e_flood_s2: "On those three floods the radar flood agreed with Sentinel-2 water at 0.21–0.38 (F1, where 1 is full overlap). Radar and optical images see different things at different times, so this is not all error, but read the result with care.", dd_e_flood_now: "In this run {c}% of the radar flood in the clear part was water on Sentinel-2 as well.", dd_e_flood_nos2: "No clear Sentinel-2 image was found this time, so the radar flood could not be checked against another source.", dd_e_px: "Edges follow the {s} m pixels: an edge may be off by up to one pixel ({s} m).", dd_e_fire_app: "Against the Google Earth Engine App on three fires the burned area was 0.5–9% smaller (357 and 359, 223 and 244, 153 and 160 km²); agreement with MODIS (F1) 0.60–0.77. On two areas without fire 1.6–2.0 km² of false burn appeared.", dd_e_fire_now: "This time {c}% of the area Sentinel-2 marks as burned lies inside the MODIS burned area (MODIS is 500 m and misses small fires).", dd_e_fire_none: "No burned area was found this time.", dd_e_cloud: "Cloud or missing images left {a} km² ({p}%) without a result.", dd_e_dust_test: "Tested on 3 known dust storms and 2 calm days, the check flagged all 3 storms and wrongly called the calm day of 2021-07-20 “some dust may rise”. With so few cases this is an example, not an error rate.", dd_e_dust_pts: "Wind and precipitation come from 9 points of the area, so the wind between them may differ.", dd_m_flood: "Method: water where the after/before ratio of 50 m means of Sentinel-1 VH is above {th} (UN-SPIDER); permanent water (JRC), slopes above 5° and groups under 800 m² removed; checked against Sentinel-2 MNDWI > 0 within 3 days.", dd_m_fire: "Method: NBR median of the 4 clearest Sentinel-2 passes; dNBR in the classes of Key and Benson (2006); for spring fires the pre-fire image from the previous autumn; MODIS MCD64A1 by burn day, active fire from MOD14A1/MYD14A1.", dd_m_dust: "Method: ground classed by NDVI on the clearest Sentinel-2 images of the last 30 days; hourly Open-Meteo wind, precipitation, snow depth and CAMS dust at 9 points; the conclusion comes from what was measured, with no new score.", dd_no_poly: "No area large enough for a polygon was found (small pieces are in the raster).", dd_pieces: "pieces", dd_period: "Period", dd_bbox: "box: west, south, east, north", dd_pixel: "Pixel size", dd_m: "m", dd_verdict: "Conclusion", dd_classes: "Area of each class", dd_class_poly: "{n} polygons, small pieces {s} km²", dd_small: "Pieces under {a} m² are left out of the polygon files (their area is in the small_km2 field); the rasters hold every pixel.", dd_warn: "Keep in mind", dd_open_classes: "Class GeoTIFF (*_classes.tif): ArcGIS Pro, QGIS and ENVI open it with its colours; class names and areas are in the table of the .tif.aux.xml. NoData = {nd}. The .tfw and .prj repeat the placement (WGS 84, EPSG:4326).", dd_open_values: "Value GeoTIFF (*_values.tif), missing values −9999. Bands:", dd_fields: "Polygon fields: code class code, cls_mn/cls_en name, area_km2 area (km²), share_pct share of the part with data, n_poly number of polygons, small_km2 area of the small pieces left out, period, px_m pixel size (m).", dd_open_png: "PNG image: next to its .pgw, .prj and .aux.xml files it lands in the right place in ArcGIS Pro and QGIS (WGS 84, EPSG:4326).", dd_footer: "Not an official warning: follow NAMEM and NEMA for decisions. Computed in the user's browser from open data of Microsoft Planetary Computer and Open-Meteo.", dd_tip_tif: "Class raster (with colours and names) and value raster: ArcGIS Pro, QGIS, ENVI", dd_tip_shp: "Polygons of each class with area: ArcGIS Pro, QGIS, ENVI", dd_tip_kmz: "Picture laid over the ground in Google Earth", dd_tip_png: "Placed picture: PNG, PGW, PRJ", dd_tip_all: "All files in one ZIP", dd_of_box: "of the whole area", dd_small_all: "The polygon files hold every piece, down to single pixels.",
